-- OmniTap per-packet metadata.
-- Normative: In standard OmniTap (Native) streams, this object is carried inside the EPB it describes as a copyable
-- Custom Binary Option (option code 2989 / 0x0BAD). In OmniTap-Lua compatibility streams (for older or unmodified
-- Wireshark/TShark installations running only the Lua dissector without the compiled asn2wrs C dissector), it is
-- carried in a standalone copyable OmniTap Custom Block (0x00000BAD). Both stream flavors carry the exact same
-- COER(OmniTapObject) bytes and are losslessly convertible with scripts/OmniLuazer.py.
-- Normative: The EPB it describes SHALL carry the standard epb_packetid option (Wireshark frame.packet_id) equal to
-- (sessionTag * 2^32) + recordId, where sessionTag is the SessionTag of the capture session.
-- Normative: Readers SHALL associate this object with the EPB whose epb_packetid equals (sessionTag * 2^32) + recordId,
-- using the sessionTag and recordId carried in this object. In OmniTap-Lua streams, association SHALL NOT use block
-- order, EPB ordinal, or Wireshark frame number. If no EPB matches, readers SHALL report this object as unassociated and
-- SHALL NOT guess.
-- Normative: Duplicates. mergecap and editcap can leave two copies of the same object, or two EPBs with the same
-- epb_packetid, in one file; in OmniTap-Lua streams, editcap -d removes duplicate EPBs but not duplicate Custom Blocks.
-- If several objects of this kind name the same (sessionTag, recordId) and their COER payloads are byte-for-byte
-- identical, readers SHALL treat them as one object. If the payloads differ, readers SHALL NOT choose between them and
-- SHALL report the packet as having conflicting metadata. The same applies to several EPBs with one epb_packetid: if
-- their packet data and timestamps are identical they are one packet; otherwise the packet identifier is ambiguous and
-- readers SHALL report this object as unassociated.
-- Normative: In OmniTap-Lua streams, producers SHOULD write this block immediately before the EPB it describes.
-- Adjacency is a convenience for simple readers, not an association key. A chronological mergecap merge separates most
-- Custom Blocks from their EPBs, whereas in OmniTap (Native) streams the option travels inside the EPB.
-- Normative: Every OmniTap object identifies its own packet or session, so it can be selected, filtered, and exported.
-- A reference dissector SHALL publish the packet identifier of each object as a display-filter field; see
-- 00-OmniTap-Requirements.md.
-- Normative: Facts already carried by standard pcapng fields are not repeated in OmniTap: packet direction and
-- link-layer error flags (epb_flags; Wireshark frame.packet_flags_*), capture interface (EPB Interface ID;
-- frame.interface_id), and packet timestamp (EPB timestamp; frame.time).
-- Normative: A packet identifier names one captured observation of a packet in one capture session. When independent
-- devices capture the same on-air packet, each device has its own capture session, so the observations carry different
-- packet identifiers although their packet bytes are identical. Readers SHALL NOT compare or merge metadata across
-- sessions.
-- Normative: The technologyMetadata alternative SHALL name the same RAT as the technology of the Interface Context of the
-- packet's interface, if there is one. A reader that finds a mismatch SHALL treat this object as authoritative for the
-- packet and SHOULD report the mismatch.
-- Rationale: The IETF OPSAWG pcapng specification describes a Custom Option (section 3.5.1) as the place for custom data
-- related to a block, and a Custom Block (section 4.8) as a container for custom data that is not part of another block.
-- OmniTap (Native) follows that guidance directly by placing Packet Metadata in EPB Custom Binary Option 2989, which
-- keeps packet and metadata atomically bound through editcap, mergecap, deduplication, and GUI packet exports. Because
-- stock Wireshark's Lua API exposes standalone Custom Blocks but not EPB Custom Binary Options, OmniTap-Lua places the
-- same COER(OmniTapObject) payload in a standalone Custom Block (0x00000BAD), and scripts/OmniLuazer.py converts
-- losslessly between the two flavors; see 00-OmniTap-Architecture.md.
OmniTap-Packet-Metadata { 1 3 6 1 4 1 32473 omniTap(1) packetMetadata(20) major-version-1(1) minor-version-0(0) }
DEFINITIONS AUTOMATIC TAGS ::= BEGIN
EXPORTS ALL;
IMPORTS

   RecordId,
   SessionTag,
   PayloadDescriptor
      FROM OmniTap-Common

   TimeQuality
      FROM OmniTap-Time

   ObserverLocation,
   RadioObservation
      FROM OmniTap-Observer

   LteV2xMetadata
      FROM OmniTap-RAT-LTE-V2X

   NrV2xMetadata
      FROM OmniTap-RAT-NR-V2X

   EtsiItsG5Metadata
      FROM OmniTap-RAT-ETSI-ITSG5

   AribT109Metadata
      FROM OmniTap-RAT-ARIB-T109

   AnalysisAnnotations
      FROM OmniTap-Supplemental-Result

   OptionalExtensions
      FROM OmniTap-Extensions
;

OmniTapPacketMetadata ::= SEQUENCE {

   -- Normative: Per-session logical-packet counter. SHALL equal the low 32 bits of the associated EPB's
   -- epb_packetid. Unique within the capture session, including across all ring-buffer files of that session;
   -- producers SHALL NOT restart it when a new ring-buffer file begins. Supplemental Results reference the packet
   -- by (sessionId, recordId).
   --
   recordId             RecordId,


   -- Normative: SHALL equal the high 32 bits of the associated EPB's epb_packetid. With recordId it forms the packet
   -- identifier, so the object names its packet whether it is carried inside the EPB as Custom Binary Option 2989
   -- (OmniTap Native) or in a standalone Custom Block (OmniTap-Lua).
   -- Rationale: In OmniTap-Lua streams, a tool that merges or filters files can separate the Custom Block from its
   -- EPB, and files from different sessions can contain the same recordId. Carrying sessionTag inside Packet Metadata
   -- keeps the COER(OmniTapObject) payload identical across OmniTap (Native) and OmniTap-Lua. This costs 32 bits per
   -- packet.
   --
   sessionTag           SessionTag,


   payload              PayloadDescriptor     OPTIONAL,   -- Native EPB packet-payload classification; absence makes no assertion.

   -- Dynamic timestamping-clock quality applicable at the associated EPB timestamp.
   --
   timeQuality          TimeQuality           OPTIONAL,


   -- Location of the capture platform at the associated EPB timestamp, for either packet direction.
   --
   observerLocation     ObserverLocation      OPTIONAL,


   -- Radio measurements and configuration for the associated packet; each field says which direction it
   -- applies to. Direction itself comes only from the EPB's epb_flags.
   --
   radio                RadioObservation      OPTIONAL,


   technologyMetadata   TechnologyMetadata    OPTIONAL,   -- RAT-specific metadata for the associated packet.


   -- Analysis known at the moment this metadata was written. Analysis that becomes available later belongs in a
   -- Supplemental Result, not here.
   --
   analysis             AnalysisAnnotations   OPTIONAL,


   optionalExtensions   OptionalExtensions    OPTIONAL,   -- Optional regional or third-party metadata for the packet.

   ...
}

TechnologyMetadata ::= CHOICE {
   lteV2x      LteV2xMetadata,      -- LTE-V2X-specific metadata.

   nrV2x       NrV2xMetadata,       -- NR-V2X-specific metadata.

   etsiItsG5   EtsiItsG5Metadata,   -- ETSI ITS-G5 metadata; Radiotap remains authoritative for 802.11 PHY/radio.

   aribT109    AribT109Metadata,    -- ITS Connect (ARIB STD-T109) metadata.

   ...
}

END

